 ## Key Design Decisions
 
 - **Commands over HTTPS, one authoritative board per event.** Editors submit commands to the server, which assigns each committed edit one event revision. Peer-to-peer or browser-side merging was rejected: the tenets require one authoritative revision regardless of delivery route, and offline editing is a non-goal.
+- **Deliver committed changes over self-hosted WebSockets.** The server pushes each committed revision to connected browsers over socket connections it owns. Five-second polling meets the freshness target but adds repeated reads and a few seconds of lag; a hosted realtime service offers low-latency delivery with less connection operation but carries recurring fees and provider coupling. Self-hosted sockets provide subsecond updates without tying the board to a realtime vendor, accepting connection-capacity and reconnect operation as the cost. See `docs/decisions/delivery-approach.md`.
